+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Twelve shared tablets can be offline for 48 hours, and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. The record store therefore receives edits that were made from the same starting state without knowledge of each other, sometimes days apart. It has to say what the visit's current value is when that happens.
+
+The answer shapes more than the record store. It fixes what a tablet must remember about the state an edit started from and what it sends, what a coordinator is asked to review, and which visits can enter the partner's Friday CSV. Tablet-sync and review-export are both sized around it, so reversing the choice later means reworking all three nodes and the tablets' local data.
+
+If the choice is wrong in one direction, coordinators carry review work the system could have avoided and visits miss exports. If it is wrong in the other, an exported visit can hold a combination of values that no worker observed and no coordinator looked at.
+
+Every option keeps each original submission, with its author and receipt time, and none treats arrival order as authority. Those hold regardless and do not separate the options.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates either option. In particular, nothing requires that every combined record receive manual approval, so automatic merging is admissible.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Scientific coherence of the current value** — major. A visit's fields are not independent: a species identification and its cover estimate are one observation, and the server cannot infer every such relationship from field names. The non-goal *automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent* means the system has no way to check a combination after making it. A current value should be something a person recorded or accepted as a whole.
+- **Inspectability of an exported value** — major. From the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*. Someone questioning a CSV value should be able to follow it back to submissions without understanding machinery.
+- **Review workload and export readiness** — major. From the goal *make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export* and the fact that two coordinators do all review. Work that reaches the queue delays a visit's export until someone attends to it.
+- **Maintenance burden** — moderate. From the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. Logic whose correctness depends on domain rules needs someone to own those rules as the survey protocol changes.
+- **Tablet storage and submission size** — minor. About 6,000 visits a season across twelve tablets; neither option strains this, but they differ.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Every submission is a complete visit and names the revision its author started from. When two submissions start from the same revision, both stand intact as competing revisions and the visit is unresolved until a coordinator resolves it. The record store never combines fields from different submissions. An unresolved visit has no current value.
+
+- Scientific coherence: **strong**. Every current value is one worker's whole submission or a coordinator's resolution.
+- Inspectability: **strong**. A value traces to one revision, and through at most a chain of resolutions to the revisions each one settled.
+- Review workload and export readiness: **weak**. Any two independent edits to a visit create review work, including edits to unrelated fields such as an observer name and a missing species. Those visits stay out of the export until resolved.
+- Maintenance burden: **strong**. One rule, with no knowledge of field types or of the survey protocol.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **strong**. A tablet keeps one revision ID per visit and sends the visit.
+
+Summary: coordinators' time is spent to guarantee that nothing is combined unseen.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Every submission carries the base state its author started from and the changes made to it. When two submissions start from the same state, changes to different fields are combined automatically into a new current value; changes to the same field go to review. Combining needs a rule per kind of field: scalars such as the observation date, the observer list, and species entries, where an entry's identification and its cover estimate may or may not be treated as one field.
+
+- Scientific coherence: **partial**. Combinations are correct wherever the rules model a relationship and can be wrong wherever they do not; a combined value is one no person reviewed.
+- Inspectability: **partial**. With field-level provenance each field traces to a submission, but the visit as a whole traces to a rule application rather than to a person's judgment.
+- Review workload and export readiness: **strong**. Only overlapping changes reach a coordinator, and visits with disjoint edits remain exportable.
+- Maintenance burden: **weak**. The merge rules are domain logic that must be kept in step with the fields and with the team's understanding of which fields belong together.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **partial**. A tablet keeps a base snapshot per edited visit and sends structured changes.
+
+Summary: review volume is cut by accepting combinations the system cannot check.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions.
+
+The two options split the major criteria: revisions are strong on coherence and inspectability and weak on review workload; merging is the reverse. The decision turns on which failure is worse. A larger review queue is visible, bounded by how often workers actually correct the same visit, and costs coordinator time. A wrong automatic combination is invisible by construction: it passes validation, enters the reviewed CSV, and reaches the science partner as an observation nobody made. The team accepts the larger queue to rule that out. The maintenance criterion points the same way: merge rules need an owner the team does not have.
+
+This is taken against the workload side of the goal *make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export* and in favor of the tenets *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+
+### When to reconsider
+
+Field-aware merging remains the credible alternative, and the case for it is entirely the review queue. How often independent edits to one visit actually occur has not been measured. Reopen the decision if:
+
+- unresolved visits regularly miss the Friday export because two coordinators cannot clear the queue in time;
+- the queue is dominated by pairs of edits that coordinators resolve by accepting both changes unaltered, which is the work a merge would have done; or
+- the team gains someone able to own merge rules, or the survey protocol gains an explicit statement of which fields form one observation, removing the coherence objection for the fields it covers.
+
+A narrower step in that direction, short of full merging, is to settle a single agreed class of harmless disjoint edits automatically. It carries the same objection at smaller scale and should be weighed the same way.
+
+Because every original submission is kept intact, moving to merging later loses no data. What it costs is the tablets' local format, the submission envelope, and the review screen.
+
+### What this obligates downstream
+
+- **tablet-sync** records, for each visit it holds, the revision its local copy started from, and sends complete visits rather than changes. It must be able to tell a worker that a submitted edit is competing with another and awaiting a coordinator, and later what became of it. It does not need base snapshots or change tracking.
+- **review-export** needs a screen that compares competing revisions of a visit as wholes and lets a coordinator resolve them. Its queue will include visits whose edits do not conflict in any field. It holds unresolved visits out of the partner CSV, since such a visit has no current value, and should make the number held out visible before Friday. It does not need field-level provenance.
+- Both take their concurrency semantics from docs/intent/record-store/record-store-design.md.
