+
+Tablet-sync and review-export build on the contract defined here. Tablet-sync sizes its local storage and retry behavior around the submission envelope and its outcomes. Review-export sizes its coordinator interface and export eligibility around the unresolved-visit representation and the resolution operation. Both link back to this node rather than define their own concurrency semantics.
 
 ## Architecture
 
-The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, submission-history retrieval, and review-outcome operations. Persistence keeps original submissions, their authors, and server receipt times. A submission carries an idempotency key so a connection failure does not create another edit on retry.
+The record store exposes authenticated submission, visit retrieval, revision-history retrieval, an unresolved-visit listing, and a resolution operation. Persistence keeps every accepted submission as originally sent, with its author and server receipt time. Nothing accepted is later edited or deleted.
+
+A visit's scientific fields are plot reference, observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names, so it treats the visit as the unit of edit and of review.
+
+## Revisions
+
+A **revision** is one accepted submission: a complete set of a visit's scientific fields, as its author intended the whole visit to read. Each revision has a client-generated UUID, so a tablet can base a second offline edit on a first one it has not yet submitted.
+
+Each revision names its **base revision**: the revision its author started from. The first revision of a visit has no base.
+
+A revision is **superseded** when a later accepted revision names it as its base, or when a resolution names it among the revisions it resolves. A revision that is not superseded is a **head**.
+
+A visit is in one of two states, determined only by its heads:
+
+- `current` — exactly one head. That head is the visit's current revision.
+- `unresolved` — two or more heads. These are the visit's **competing revisions**. The visit has no current revision.
+
+The state does not depend on the order in which submissions arrived, on who authored them, or on which fields differ. Two revisions based on the same revision compete even when they change unrelated fields, when they have the same author, and when their payloads are identical.
+
+An edit based on a competing revision supersedes that revision and takes its place among the competing revisions; the visit stays unresolved. An edit whose base is already superseded is accepted and becomes a head, so a visit that was current, including one a coordinator has resolved, becomes unresolved when a late edit from an older starting point arrives.
+
+## Submission
+
+### Envelope
+
+| Field | Content |
+|---|---|
+| `visit_id` | Client-generated UUID of the visit |
+| `revision_id` | Client-generated UUID of this revision; also the idempotency key |
+| `base_revision_id` | The revision the author started from; absent for a visit's first revision |
+| `payload` | Complete scientific fields: plot reference, observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes |
+
+The author is the authenticated worker, taken from the session, not from the envelope.
+
+### Outcomes
+
+A submission is either accepted or rejected. A rejected submission is not stored; the tablet keeps the draft and the reason.
+
+| Outcome | Condition |
+|---|---|
+| Accepted | The submission is stored as a revision. The response carries the visit state after acceptance (`current` or `unresolved`) and the head revision IDs at that moment. |
+| Rejected: `unauthenticated` | No authenticated worker |
+| Rejected: `invalid_payload` | A field has the wrong type, a required field is missing, or the plot reference is not a catalog plot ID. An archived plot is a valid reference. |
+| Rejected: `unknown_base` | `base_revision_id` names a revision the server has not accepted. Tablets submit a chain of offline edits oldest first, so this means an earlier link was not accepted. |
+| Rejected: `base_visit_mismatch` | `base_revision_id` names a revision of a different visit |
+| Rejected: `revision_id_reused` | `revision_id` matches an accepted revision whose envelope differs |
+
+A submission is never rejected because its base is superseded or because the visit is unresolved.
+
+A submission whose `revision_id` matches an accepted revision with an identical envelope returns the original acceptance response and stores nothing. That response describes the visit as it was at first acceptance; a tablet that needs the present state retrieves the visit.
+
+Submissions for one visit are applied one at a time. Two submissions on the same base that arrive together are both accepted; the first is told `current` and the second `unresolved`, and the resulting visit is the same whichever arrived first.
+
+## Retrieval
+
+### Visit
+
+| Field | Content |
+|---|---|
+| `visit_id` | The visit |
+| `state` | `current` or `unresolved` |
+| `current_revision` | Present only when `state` is `current`: the head revision, in full |
+| `competing_revisions` | Present only when `state` is `unresolved`: every head revision, in full |
+| `shared_base_revision_id` | Present only when `state` is `unresolved`: the most recent revision from which every competing revision descends; absent when they share none |
+
+A revision in full is its `revision_id`, `base_revision_id`, payload, author ID, and server receipt time; a resolution also carries the IDs of the revisions it resolved. A revision descends from its base and, for a resolution, from each revision it resolved.
+
+Retrieval of an unresolved visit never nominates one competing revision as preferred, and never returns an earlier revision as a stand-in current value.
+
+### Revision history
+
+History retrieval returns every accepted revision of a visit in full, each with how it stands: head, superseded by a named later edit, or superseded by a named resolution. This is what lets a tablet tell a worker what became of their submission, and what lets an exported value be traced back through the resolution that chose it to the original submissions.
+
+### Unresolved visits
+
+The listing returns the IDs of all visits whose state is `unresolved`, each with the server receipt time of its earliest competing revision. It is the source of the coordinators' review work.
+
+## Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves an unresolved visit by submitting a **resolution**: a revision, authored by the coordinator, that names the competing revisions it resolves instead of a base. Its payload is a complete visit. The coordinator may take one competing revision's payload unchanged or enter a payload that draws on several; either way the result is a whole visit a person has read and accepted, and the competing revisions remain stored as submitted.
+
+| Field | Content |
+|---|---|
+| `visit_id` | The unresolved visit |
+| `revision_id` | Client-generated UUID of the resolution; also the idempotency key |
+| `resolves` | The IDs of the competing revisions the coordinator reviewed |
+| `payload` | Complete scientific fields |
 
